13. React Debugging and Component Tests

Browser Console Errors and React Warnings:
Console Errors: Standard JavaScript runtime exceptions (e.g., TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined) that crash or halt script execution.
React Warnings: Development-only notices logged by React in the browser console. They highlight potential bugs and performance anti-patterns before they cause runtime failures (e.g., missing key props in rendered lists, switching inputs from uncontrolled to controlled, or invalid DOM attributes like using class instead of className).
React Developer Tools:
A browser extension (available for Chrome and Firefox) that adds two main panels to Developer Tools: Components and Profiler.
The Components tree allows you to inspect the current hierarchy of React components, view and live-edit their props, state, and hooks, and locate source files directly.
The Profiler records re-render durations to identify unnecessary renders and performance bottlenecks.
What a Component Test Checks:
Component tests verify that a unit of UI renders expected content given specific props/state and responds correctly to simulated user interactions (such as button clicks, typing into form inputs, or submitting forms).
Testing Behaviour vs. Implementation Details:
Implementation Details: Internal state variable names, CSS class names, component structural tags (<div> vs <section>), or internal handler function names.
User Behaviour: Visible text content, accessible roles (button, heading), user inputs, and output callbacks triggered by user actions.
Why it matters: Tests that rely on implementation details break whenever you refactor internal code without actually changing how the application works for the end user. Testing output and accessibility (the behaviour) ensures tests remain resilient and reliably guarantee app functionality.

